== Chapter 5

#align(center)[#smallcaps(all: true)[THE KNOWLEDGE OF GOD CONSPICUOUS IN THE CREATION, AND CONTINUAL GOVERNMENT OF THE WORLD.]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[This chapter has two parts. 1. The first, which takes up the first ten sections, divides all the works of God into two great classes, and explains the knowledge of God as it is displayed in each class. The one class is treated in the first six sections, and the other in the four that follow. 2. The second part of the chapter shows that, because of the extreme dullness of men, these manifestations of God, however clear, lead to no useful result. This second part begins at the eleventh section and runs to the end of the chapter.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Sections.]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[1\. The invisible and incomprehensible essence of God made visible, to a certain extent, in his works.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[2\. This is declared by the first class of works—namely, the wonderful motions of the heavens and the earth, the symmetry of the human body, and the connection of its parts; in short, the various objects that meet every eye.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[3\. This is shown most of all in the structure of the human body.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[4\. The shameful ingratitude of disregarding God, who shows himself within us in so many ways. The still more shameful ingratitude of contemplating the gifts of the soul without rising to Him who gave them. No objection can be built on any supposed organic structure in the soul.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[5\. The powers and actions of the soul a proof that it exists apart from the body. Proofs of the soul’s immortality. The objection that the whole world is given life by one soul. Reply to the objection. Its impiety.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[6\. Conclusion from what has been said—namely, that the omnipotence, eternity, and goodness of God may be learned from the first class of works, that is, those that follow the ordinary course of nature.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[7\. The second class of works—namely, those beyond the ordinary course of nature—gives clear evidence of the perfections of God, especially his goodness, justice, and mercy.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[8\. Also of his providence, power, and wisdom.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[9\. Proofs and illustrations of the divine Majesty. Their use—namely, gaining the knowledge of God together with true piety.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[10\. The knowledge of God tends to inspire the righteous with the hope of a future life, and to remind the wicked of the punishments reserved for them. It tends also to keep alive in the hearts of the righteous a sense of the divine goodness.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[11\. The second part of the chapter, which describes the dullness of learned and unlearned alike in ascribing the whole order of things, and the wonderful arrangements of divine Providence, to fortune.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[12\. Hence polytheism, with all its abominations, and the endless and irreconcilable opinions of the philosophers about God.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[13\. All are guilty of revolt from God, corrupting pure religion by following either general custom or the impious consent of antiquity.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[14\. Though the wonderful glories of creation shine on us, we do not cease to follow our own ways.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[15\. Our conduct is altogether inexcusable: the dullness of perception is our own fault, while both the structure and the government of the world fully remind us of the true path.]

#v(0.8em)

1\. #smallcaps[Since] the perfection of blessedness consists in the knowledge of God, he has been pleased, so that no one might be shut out from the way to happiness, not only to place in our minds that seed of religion of which we have already spoken, but also to reveal himself in the whole structure of the universe, and to set himself daily before our view, in such a way that we cannot open our eyes without being compelled to see him. His essence, indeed, is incomprehensible, so that his Godhead lies far beyond the reach of all human senses; but on each of his works his glory is engraved in letters so bright, so distinct, and so splendid that no one, however dull and unlearned, can plead ignorance as an excuse. So the Psalmist, with perfect truth, exclaims, “He wraps Himself in light as with a garment” (Ps 104:2). It is as if he had said that God first appeared clothed in visible attire when, in the creation of the world, he unfurled those glorious banners on which, whichever way we turn, we see his perfections visibly portrayed. In the same place the Psalmist aptly compares the outstretched heavens to his royal tent, and speaks of Him “laying the beams of His chambers in the waters, making the clouds His chariot, walking on the wings of the wind,” and sending out the winds and lightnings as his swift messengers. And because the glory of his power and wisdom shines more brightly in the sky above, the heavens are often called his palace. And, first, wherever you turn your eyes, there is no part of the world, however small, that does not show at least some sparks of his glory; and you cannot take in this vast and beautiful structure, as it stretches all around, without being overwhelmed by the immense weight of glory. That is why the author of the Epistle to the Hebrews elegantly calls the visible worlds images of the invisible (Heb 11:3): the harmonious arrangement of the world serves us as a kind of mirror in which we may see God, who is otherwise invisible. For the same reason the Psalmist gives the heavenly bodies a language that all nations understand (Ps 19:1), for the Godhead is shown there too clearly to escape the notice of any people, however obtuse. The apostle Paul, stating this still more clearly, says, “What may be known about God is plain to them, because God has made it plain to them. For since the creation of the world His invisible things, His eternal power and divine nature, have been clearly seen, being understood from His workmanship” (Rom 1:19, 20); so that all, to a man, see them.

2\. To bear witness to his wonderful wisdom, both the heavens and the earth present us with countless proofs: not only those more hidden proofs which astronomy, medicine, and all the natural sciences are designed to bring to light, but proofs that force themselves on the notice of the most unlettered peasant, who cannot open his eyes without seeing them. It is true, indeed, that those who are more or less closely acquainted with these liberal arts are helped by them and enabled to gain a deeper insight into the secret workings of divine wisdom. Yet no man, even if he knows nothing of them, is prevented from discerning such proofs of creative wisdom as may well make him break out in wonder at the Creator. To investigate the motions of the heavenly bodies, to determine their positions, measure their distances, and note their properties requires skill and more careful study; and where these are so employed, as the Providence of God is more fully unfolded by them, so it is right that the mind should take a somewhat loftier flight to behold his glory.#footnote[Augustinus: Astrologia magnum religiosis argumentum, tormentumque curiosis. \[Astronomy is a great proof to the religious, and a torment to the overcurious.\] (Beveridge’s note.)] Still, no one who has the use of his eyes can be ignorant of the divine skill shown so plainly in the endless variety, and yet the distinct and well-ordered array, of the heavenly host; and so it is plain that the Lord has given every man abundant proofs of his wisdom. The same is true of the structure of the human frame. To trace the connection of its parts, its symmetry and beauty, with the skill of a Galen (Lib. De Usu Partium), requires uncommon sharpness of mind; and yet all men acknowledge that the human body bears on its face such proofs of ingenious design as are enough to proclaim the wonderful wisdom of its Maker.

3\. So some of the philosophers#footnote[See Aristot. Hist. Anim. lib. i. c. 17; Macrob. in Somn. Scip lib. 2 c. 12; Boeth. De Definitione. (Beveridge’s note.)] have rightly called man a #emph[microcosm] (#emph[miniature world]), since he is a rare example of divine power, wisdom, and goodness, and contains within himself wonders enough to occupy our minds, if only we are willing to give them our attention. Paul, accordingly, after reminding the Athenians that even the blind “might grope for God and find Him,” at once adds that “He is not far from each one of us” (Acts 17:27), since every man has within himself undoubted evidence of the heavenly grace by which he lives and moves and has his being. But if, to grasp God, we need not go outside ourselves, what excuse can there be for the sloth of any man who will not take the trouble to go down into himself to find Him? For the same reason, too, David, after briefly celebrating the wonderful name and glory of God as displayed everywhere, at once exclaims, “What is man that You are mindful of him?” and again, “From the mouths of children and infants You have ordained strength” (Ps 8:2, 4). So he declares not only that the human race is a bright mirror of the Creator’s works, but that infants still hanging at their mothers’ breasts have tongues eloquent enough to proclaim his glory with no need of other orators. And so he does not hesitate to bring them forward into the battle as fully armed to refute the madness of those who, in their devilish pride, would gladly blot out the name of God. Hence, too, comes the passage which Paul quotes from Aratus, “We are His offspring” (Acts 17:28), for the excellent gifts with which God has endowed us testify that he is our Father. In the same way, from natural instinct and, as it were, as experience dictated, the heathen poets called him the father of men. Indeed, no one will freely and willingly devote himself to the service of God unless he has first tasted his fatherly love and been drawn by it to love and reverence Him.

4\. But here the shameful ingratitude of men comes to light. They have in their own persons a workshop where countless works of God are carried on, and a storehouse filled with treasures of priceless value; yet instead of bursting out in his praise, as they are bound to do, they are all the more puffed up and swollen with pride. They feel how wonderfully God is working in them, and their own experience tells them of the great variety of gifts they owe to his generosity. Whether they like it or not, they cannot help knowing that these are proofs of his Godhead, and yet inwardly they suppress them. They have no need to go outside themselves, provided they do not take for their own what has been given them from heaven, and so bury in the earth the light meant to show God clearly to their minds. Yet today the earth bears on her bosom many monstrous minds—minds that are not afraid to use the seed of Deity planted in human nature as a means of suppressing the name of God. Can anything be more detestable than this madness in man, who finds God a hundred times over in his body and his soul, and then makes this very excellence a pretext for denying that there is a God? He will not say that chance has made him differ from the beasts that perish; but, drawing the veil of nature over everything, and making nature the architect of the universe, he suppresses the name of God. He sees such exquisite workmanship in each of his members, from his mouth and eyes down to the very tips of his nails; here, too, he puts nature in the place of God. But above all, the swift motions of the soul, its noble faculties and rare gifts, point to a divinity that would not easily let itself be hidden, if the Epicureans, like so many Cyclopes, did not use this height as a vantage ground from which to wage a bolder war on God. Are so many treasures of heavenly wisdom employed in guiding such a worm as man, and shall the whole universe be denied the same privilege? To hold that there are organs in the soul corresponding to each of the parts of the body is so far from obscuring the glory of God that it rather sets it in a clearer light. Let Epicurus tell us what meeting of atoms, cooking food and drink, can turn one part into waste and another into blood, and make each of the members do its own work as carefully as if they were so many souls acting by common consent in governing one body.

5\. But my business at present is not with that pigsty. I would rather deal with those who, carried away by absurd subtleties, like to give a crooked twist to that frigid doctrine of Aristotle and use it both to do away with the immortality of the soul and to rob God of his rights. On the pretext that the faculties of the soul are organic, they chain it to the body as if it could not exist apart from it, while by heaping praises on nature they do all they can to suppress the name of God. But there is no ground for holding that the powers of the soul are confined to serving the functions of the body. What has the body to do with your measuring the heavens, counting the stars, determining their sizes, their distances from one another, the speed at which they move, and the orbits they trace? I do not deny that astronomy has its use; all I mean to show is that these lofty investigations are carried on not by organic symmetry but by the faculties of the soul itself, altogether apart from the body. The single example I have given will suggest many others to the reader. The swift and versatile movements of the soul as it ranges from heaven to earth, joins the future to the past, keeps the memory of former years, and even forms creations of its own—its skill, too, in making astonishing discoveries and inventing so many wonderful arts—are sure marks of divinity in man. What shall we say of its activity while the body sleeps: its many thoughts turning this way and that, its many useful ideas, its many solid arguments, even its sense of things yet to come? What can we say but that man carries about with him a stamp of immortality that can never be erased? But how is it possible for man to be divine and yet not acknowledge his Creator? Shall we, by a power of judgment planted in our breast, tell justice from injustice, and yet there be no judge in heaven? Shall some remnant of intelligence stay with us in sleep, and yet no God keep watch in governing the world? Shall we be thought the inventors of so many arts and useful things, so that God is cheated of his praise, when experience shows plainly enough that whatever we have is handed out to us in unequal measures by another hand? The talk of certain people about a secret inspiration that gives life to the whole world is not only shallow but altogether profane. Such people are delighted with this famous passage of Virgil:#footnote[Aeneid, 6 724, sq. See Calvin on Acts 17:28 Manil. lib. 1. Astron. (partly Beveridge’s note.)]

#quote[“Know, first, that heaven, and earth’s compacted frame,
And flowing waters, and the starry flame,
And both the radiant lights, one common soul
Inspires and feeds—and animates the whole.
This active mind, infused through all the space,
Unites and mingles with the mighty mass:
Hence, men and beasts the breath of life obtain,
And birds of air, and monsters of the main.
Th’ ethereal vigor is in all the same,
And every soul is filled with equal flame.”#footnote[Dryden’s Virgil, Æneid, Book 6, l. 980-990. (Beveridge’s note.)]]

The meaning of all this is that the world, which was made to display the glory of God, is its own creator. For the same poet, in another place,#footnote[Georgic 4. 220. Plat. in Tim. Arist. lib. 1 De Animo. See also Metaph. lib. 1. Merc. Trismeg. in Pimandro. (partly Beveridge’s note.)] adopts a view common to Greeks and Latins alike:

#quote[“Hence to the bee some sages have assigned
A portion of the God, and heavenly mind;
For God goes forth, and spreads throughout the whole,
Heaven, earth, and sea, the universal soul;
Each, at its birth, from him all beings share,
Both man and brute, the breath of vital air;
To him return, and, loosed from earthly chain,
Fly whence they sprung, and rest in God again;
Spurn at the grave, and, fearless of decay,
Dwell in high heaven, and star th’ ethereal way.”#footnote[Dryden’s Virgil, Book 4. 1. 252-262. (Beveridge’s note.)]]

Here we see how far that barren speculation about a universal mind that animates and invigorates the world is fit to give birth to piety and nourish it in our minds. We have still clearer proof of this in the profane verses which that impure dog Lucretius wrote, drawing them from the same principle.#footnote[He maintains, at the beginning of the First Book, that nothing is produced from nothing, but that all things are formed out of certain primary materials. He also twists the ordinary course of generation into an argument against the existence of God. In the Fifth Book, however, he admits that the world was born and will die. (Beveridge’s note.)] Their plain aim is to make a shadowy deity, and so to banish the true God whom we ought to fear and worship. I admit, indeed, that the saying “Nature is God” may be used piously, if it comes from a pious mind; but because it is inaccurate and harsh (Nature being, more properly, the order that has been established by God), it does harm, in matters so weighty and calling for special reverence, to confuse the Deity with the lower workings of his hands.

6\. Let each of us, therefore, when he considers his own nature, remember that there is one God who governs all natures, and who, in governing them, wants us to look to him, to make him the object of our faith, worship, and invocation. Indeed, nothing could be more perverse than to enjoy those noble gifts that speak of the divine presence within us and to neglect him who, of his own good pleasure, bestows them on us. As for his power, how glorious are the displays by which he urges us to contemplate him—unless, indeed, we pretend not to know how great an energy it is that by a word sustains the boundless frame of the universe: at one time making heaven resound with thunder, sending out the scorching lightning, and setting the whole sky ablaze; at another, stirring up raging storms, and then at once, in one moment, when it pleases him, bringing a perfect calm; holding back the sea, which seems always to threaten the earth with ruin, as if it were hung in the air; now lashing it into fury by the force of the winds, now calming its rage and stilling all its waves. Here we might refer to the glowing descriptions of divine power, drawn from the works of nature, that occur throughout Scripture, but especially in the book of Job and the prophecies of Isaiah. These, however, I purposely pass over, because a better place for them will be found when I come to treat the Scriptural account of the creation.#footnote[See below, ch. 14 s. 1, 2, 20 ff. (Beveridge’s note.)] I only wish to point out here that this way of seeking God, by tracing the features of his face as they are sketched in the sky above and on the earth below, is common both to those inside and to those outside the Church. From the power of God we are naturally led to consider his eternity, since that from which all other things take their origin must necessarily be self-existent and eternal. Further, if it is asked what cause led him to create all things at first, and now moves him to preserve them, we shall find that there could be no other cause than his own goodness. But if this is the only cause, nothing more should be needed to draw out our love toward him; for, as the Psalmist reminds us, every creature shares in his mercy: “His mercy rests on all He has made” (Ps 145:9).

7\. In the second class of God’s works, namely those that happen beyond the ordinary course of nature, the evidence of his perfections is in every way just as clear. For in ruling the affairs of men he so orders the course of his providence as to declare daily, by the clearest signs, that though all share in his bounty in countless ways, the righteous are the special objects of his favor, and the wicked and profane the special objects of his severity. There can be no doubt about his punishment of crimes; and at the same time he shows unmistakably that he is the protector, and even the avenger, of innocence, by pouring out blessings on the good, helping them in their needs, soothing and comforting their griefs, relieving their sufferings, and in every way providing for their safety. And though he often allows the guilty to exult for a time unpunished, and the innocent to be tossed to and fro in adversity, and even to be wickedly and unjustly oppressed, this ought not to make us at all uncertain about the unvarying justice of all his dealings. No, the opposite conclusion should be drawn. When any one crime calls out visible signs of his anger, it must be because he hates all crimes; and, on the other hand, his leaving many crimes unpunished only proves that there is a judgment to come, when the punishment now delayed will be inflicted. In the same way, how richly he supplies us with means of contemplating his mercy when, as often happens, he goes on visiting miserable sinners with unwearied kindness, until he subdues their depravity and woos them back with more than a parent’s fondness!

8\. To this end the Psalmist (Ps 107) tells how God, in wonderful ways, often brings sudden and unexpected help to the wretched when they are almost at the brink of despair: protecting them when they stray in deserts, and at last leading them back to the right path; or supplying them with food when they are starving; or setting them free from iron fetters and foul dungeons when they are captives; or bringing them safe into harbor after shipwreck; or bringing them back from the gates of death by healing their diseases; or burning up the fields with heat and drought; or making them fruitful with the secret watering of his grace; or raising up the lowliest of the people, and casting down the mighty from their lofty seats. After bringing forward examples of this kind, the Psalmist concludes that the things men call chance events are so many proofs of divine providence, and especially of fatherly clemency, giving the righteous cause for joy and at the same time stopping the mouths of the ungodly. But since most of mankind, enslaved by error, walk blindfold in this glorious theater, he exclaims that it is a rare and singular wisdom to weigh these works of God carefully; for many who seem very sharp-sighted in other respects look on them without profit. It is indeed true that the brightest display of divine glory finds scarcely one true spectator in a hundred. Still, neither his power nor his wisdom is hidden in darkness. His power is strikingly displayed when the rage of the wicked, irresistible to all appearance, is crushed in a single moment: their arrogance is subdued, their strongest fortresses overthrown, their armor smashed to pieces, their strength broken, their schemes defeated, and they themselves fall by their own weight; and the audacity that raised itself above the heavens is hurled down to the lowest depths of the earth. On the other hand, the poor are raised up out of the dust, and the needy lifted out of the dunghill (Ps 113:7); the oppressed and afflicted are rescued in their extremity, the despairing are given new hope, the unarmed defeat the armed, the few the many, the weak the strong. The excellence of the divine wisdom is shown in dispensing everything at the right time, confounding the wisdom of the world, and catching the wise in their own craftiness (1 Cor 3:19); in short, in ordering all things in perfect accord with reason.

9\. We see that there is no need of a long and laborious chain of argument to bring out proofs that illustrate and assert the Divine Majesty. The few we have merely touched on show that they lie so close at hand on every side that we can trace them with the eye or point to them with the finger. And here we must observe again,#footnote[See ch. 2 s. 2. (Beveridge’s note.)] that the knowledge of God which we are invited to cultivate is not the kind that, content with empty speculation, merely flutters about in the brain, but a knowledge that will prove solid and fruitful wherever it is rightly grasped and rooted in the heart. The Lord is made known by his perfections. When we feel their power within us and are aware of their benefits, this knowledge must impress us far more vividly than if we merely imagined a God whose presence we never felt. Hence it is plain that in seeking God the most direct path and the fittest method is not to attempt, with presumptuous curiosity, to pry into his essence, which is to be adored rather than minutely discussed, but to contemplate him in his works, by which he draws near to us, becomes familiar, and in a manner imparts himself to us. The Apostle was referring to this when he said that we need not go far in search of him (Acts 17:27), because, by the continual working of his power, he dwells in every one of us. So David (Ps 145), after confessing that his greatness is unsearchable, goes on to recount his works, declaring that by this means his greatness will be made known. We too, therefore, ought to pursue diligently that search for God which so holds the mind rapt in wonder as, at the same time, to make an effectual impression on it. And as Augustine puts it (in Ps 144), since we cannot comprehend Him, and are, as it were, overwhelmed by his greatness, our proper course is to look at his works and so refresh ourselves with his goodness.

10\. Knowledge of this kind ought not only to stir us to worship God, but also to awaken us and lift us up to the hope of the future life. For when we observe that the displays the Lord gives of both his mercy and his severity are only begun and incomplete, we ought to conclude that they are without doubt only a prelude to greater things, whose full display is reserved for another state. Again, when we see the righteous brought into affliction by the ungodly, assailed with injuries, overwhelmed with slanders, and torn by insult and abuse, while the wicked, on the other hand, flourish, prosper, and gain ease and honor, and all this with impunity, we ought at once to conclude that there will be a future life in which iniquity will receive its punishment and righteousness its reward. Moreover, when we see that the Lord often lays his chastening rod on the righteous, we may conclude all the more surely that the unrighteous will far less escape the scourges of his anger in the end. There is a well-known saying of Augustine (De Civitat. Dei, lib. 1 c. 8): “If every sin were now visited with open punishment, it might be thought that nothing was reserved for the final judgment; and, on the other hand, if no sin were now openly punished, it might be supposed that there was no divine providence.” It must be acknowledged, then, that in each of the works of God, and especially in all of them taken together, the divine perfections are painted as in a picture, and the whole human race is invited and drawn by them to the knowledge of God, and through this knowledge to true and complete happiness. Further, though his perfections are displayed there most vividly, the only way to learn what they do in practice and where they lead is to go down into ourselves and consider how the Lord shows his life, wisdom, and power in us, and how he displays his justice, goodness, and mercy toward us. For although David (Ps 92:6) rightly complains of the utter folly of the ungodly in not weighing the deep counsels of God shown in the government of the human race, what he says elsewhere (Ps 40) is most true: that the wonders of the divine wisdom in this respect are more in number than the hairs of our head. But I leave this topic for now, as it will be considered more fully later in its own place.#footnote[Book I. c. 16, sec. 6-9. (Beveridge’s note.)]

11\. Yet however brightly God shows both himself and his immortal kingdom in the mirror of his works, so great is our stupor, so dull are we toward these bright displays, that we get no benefit from them. For as to the frame and wonderful order of the universe, how few of us, when we lift our eyes to the heavens or look out over the various regions of the earth, ever think of the Creator? Do we not rather overlook Him, and sluggishly content ourselves with the sight of his works? And then, as to the events that occur daily beyond the ordinary course of nature, how few ascribe them to the ruling providence of God—how many imagine that they are accidents produced by the blind turning of the wheel of chance? Even when, under the guidance and direction of these events, we are in a manner forced to contemplate God (as all must be at times), and are so led to form, rashly, some impression of Deity, we at once fly off to carnal dreams and depraved fictions, and so by our futility corrupt heavenly truth. In this, indeed, we differ from one another, that each of us takes up some error of his own; but we are all alike in this, that we fall away, to a man, from the one true God to monstrous fictions—a disease not confined to dull and common minds, but affecting the noblest, and those who in other respects are exceptionally sharp. How lavishly in this respect the whole body of philosophers has betrayed its stupidity and lack of sense! To say nothing of the others, whose absurdities are grosser still, how completely Plato, the soberest and most religious of them all, loses himself in his round globe!#footnote[Plato in Timaeos. See also Cic. De Nat. Deorum, lib. 1; Plut. De Philos Placitis, lib. i. (Beveridge’s note.)] What must become of the rest, when the leaders, who ought to have set them an example, make such blunders and labor under such delusions? In the same way, although the government of human affairs proves a providence too plainly to be denied, the practical result is the same as if all things were believed to be tossed up and down at the whim of chance; so prone are we to futility and error. I am still speaking of the most distinguished of the philosophers, not of the common herd, whose madness in profaning the truth of God knows no bounds.

12\. Hence that immense flood of error that has overflowed the whole world. Since each man’s mind is a kind of labyrinth, it is no wonder that not only has each nation taken up a variety of fictions, but almost every man has had his own god. To the darkness of ignorance have been added presumption and wantonness, and so there is scarcely one man to be found without some idol or phantom to stand in place of Deity. Like water gushing out of a great and abundant spring, immense crowds of gods have poured out of the human mind, as every man gives himself full license and devises some form of divinity of his own to suit his own views. There is no need here to attempt a catalogue of the superstitions that overspread the world. It would be endless; and the corruptions themselves, even if not a word were said, give abundant evidence of the blindness of the human mind. I say nothing of the rough and unlearned common people; but among the philosophers#footnote[Cicero: Qui deos esse dixerunt tanta sunt in varietate ac dissensione, ut eorum molestum sit enumerare sententias. \[Those who have said that there are gods are in such variety and disagreement that it is tiresome to list their opinions.\]—Cicero, De Nat Deorum, lib. 1 and 2. Lactant Inst. Div. lib. 1 &c. (Beveridge’s note.)] who tried by reason and learning to pierce the heavens, what shameful disagreement! The more highly any one of them was gifted, and the more he was polished by science and art, the more plausible the coloring he gave to his opinions. All of them, however, if examined more closely, will be found to be empty show. The Stoics prided themselves on their cleverness when they said#footnote[Seneca, De Benef., lib. 4 c. 7, et Natural. Quaest. lib. 1 in Praef., et lib. 2 c. 45. (Beveridge’s note.)] that the various names of God might be drawn from all the parts of nature, and yet his unity would not be divided by it: as if we were not prone enough to futility already, without being presented with an endless multitude of gods to lead us further and more grossly into error. The mystic theology of the Egyptians shows how diligently they all labored that they might not seem to be foolish without reason.#footnote[Plutarch. lib. De Iside et Osiride. (Beveridge’s note.)] Perhaps, at first glance, some show of probability might deceive the simple and unwary; but no mortal ever devised a scheme by which religion was not foully corrupted. This endless variety and confusion emboldened the Epicureans and other gross despisers of piety to cast off all sense of God. For when they saw that the wisest contradicted one another, they did not hesitate to infer from their quarrels, and from the frivolous and absurd teachings of each, that men foolishly and to no purpose brought torment on themselves by searching for a God when there is none. And they thought this inference safe, because it was better to deny God altogether, once and for all, than to invent uncertain gods and then get into quarrels without end. They argue absurdly, indeed, or rather they weave a cloak for their impiety out of human ignorance; though ignorance surely cannot take anything away from the prerogatives of God. But since all confess that there is no subject on which learned and unlearned alike differ so widely, the proper conclusion is that the human mind, which errs like this in seeking after God, is more than dull and blind in heavenly mysteries. Some praise the answer of Simonides, who, when King Hiero asked him what God was, asked for a day to think it over. When the king repeated the question the next day, he asked for two days; and after doubling the number of days again and again, he at last replied, “The longer I consider it, the darker the subject seems.”#footnote[Cicero, De Nat. Deor. lib. 1. (Beveridge’s note.)] No doubt he was wise to suspend his judgment on a matter he did not see clearly; still his answer shows that if men are taught by nature alone, instead of holding any distinct, solid, or certain knowledge, they fasten only on confused principles, and so worship an unknown God.

13\. We must also hold, then, that whoever corrupts pure religion (and this must happen to all who cling to their own opinions) departs from the one God. They will claim, no doubt, that they mean something else; but it matters little what they intend or persuade themselves to believe, since the Holy Spirit declares all to be apostates who, in the blindness of their minds, put demons in the place of God. For this reason Paul declares that the Ephesians were “without God” (Eph 2:12) until they had learned from the Gospel what it is to worship the true God. Nor must this be limited to one people, since in another place he declares in general that all men “became futile in their thinking” (Rom 1:21) after the majesty of the Creator had been revealed to them in the structure of the world. Accordingly, Scripture, to make room for the one true God, condemns all the gods celebrated among the Gentiles as lying and false, and leaves no Deity anywhere but on Mount Zion, where the special knowledge of God flourished (Hab 2:18, 20). Among the Gentiles in the time of Christ, the Samaritans certainly seemed to come nearest to true piety; yet we hear from Christ’s own mouth that they worshiped what they did not know (John 4:22); and from this it follows that they were deluded by vain errors. In short, though not all gave way to gross vices or rushed headlong into open idolatry, there was no pure and approved religion founded merely on the understanding common to mankind. A few may not have gone to all the mad lengths of the common people; still Paul’s teaching stands firm, that the wisdom of God was not grasped by the rulers of this world (1 Cor 2:8). But if the most distinguished wandered in darkness, what shall we say of the dregs? No wonder, then, that the Holy Spirit rejects as degenerate all worship of man’s devising. Any opinion that man can form about heavenly mysteries, even if it does not always give birth to a long train of errors, is still the parent of error. And even if nothing worse should come of it, this in itself is no light sin—to worship an unknown God at random. Yet we hear from our Savior’s own mouth (John 4:22) that all are guilty of this sin who have not been taught from the law who the God is that they ought to worship. And indeed the best of lawgivers went no further than to found religion on public consent. Even Socrates, in Xenophon (lib. 1 Memorabilia), praises the reply of Apollo directing every man to worship the gods according to the rites of his country and the particular custom of his own city. But what right have mortals to decide by their own authority a matter that is far above the world? Or who can so rest in the will of his forefathers or the decrees of the people as to receive a god from their hands without hesitation? Each man will hold to his own judgment sooner than submit to the dictation of others. Since, therefore, in ordering the worship of God, the custom of a city or the consent of antiquity is too feeble and fragile a bond of piety, it remains that God himself must bear witness to himself from heaven.

14\. In vain for us, therefore, does Creation hold up so many bright lamps, lit to show the glory of its Author. Though they shine on us from every side, they are wholly unable of themselves to lead us into the right path. Some sparks they do give off, certainly; but these are smothered before they can blaze out into a fuller light. That is why the apostle, in the very place where he says that the worlds are images of invisible things, adds that it is #emph[by faith] that we understand they were framed by the word of God (Heb 11:3). By this he means that the invisible Godhead is indeed represented by such displays, but that we have no eyes to see it until they are enlightened through faith by an inward revelation from God. When Paul says that what may be known of God is made plain by the creation of the world, he does not mean a manifestation that human insight can grasp (Rom 1:19); on the contrary, he shows that it goes no further than to leave us without excuse. And though he says elsewhere that we need not seek far for God, since he dwells within us (Acts 17:27), he shows in another passage how far this nearness of God avails. God, he says, in past generations “let all nations walk in their own ways. Yet He has not left Himself without witness to His goodness: He gives rain from heaven and fruitful seasons, filling the hearts of men with food and gladness” (Acts 14:16, 17). But though God is not left without a witness, while with countless and varied acts of kindness he woos men to the knowledge of himself, they still do not cease to follow their own ways, that is, their deadly errors.

15\. But though we lack the natural powers that might enable us to rise to a pure and clear knowledge of God, still, since the dullness that hinders us is within us, there is no room for excuse. We cannot plead ignorance without being convicted at the same time by our own consciences of both sloth and ingratitude. It would indeed be a strange defense for man to claim that he has no ears to hear the truth, when mute things have voices loud enough to declare it; to plead that he cannot see what things without eyes point out; to excuse himself on the ground of weakness of mind, when all things without reason are able to teach. So when we wander and go astray, we are justly shut out from every kind of excuse, because all things point to the right path. But while man must bear the guilt of corrupting the seed of divine knowledge, sown in his mind by the wonderful workmanship of nature, and of keeping it from bearing good and sound fruit, it is still most true that we are by no means sufficiently instructed by that bare and simple, yet magnificent, testimony which created things bear to the glory of their Creator. For no sooner have we gained some slight knowledge of Deity from a survey of the world than we pass by the true God and set up in his place the dreams and phantoms of our own brain, drawing away the praise of justice, wisdom, goodness, and power from the fountainhead and carrying it off elsewhere. Moreover, by our false judgment of them, we so obscure or twist his daily works as to rob both the works of their glory and their author of his due praise.
